## PART B — FLOOR/HARNESS ROBUSTNESS (mechanical, all measured the hard way)

### B1. PROCESS-DEATH-SAFE MUTATION HYGIENE
**Target:** fp_kill "MUTANT NON-RUNS" block.
**Measured:** a process restart (not a timeout) killed a battery mid-mutation: the finally-restore never ran, an INERT declared-survivor mutation stayed in the live tree keeping every suite green, plus a stale lock blocked subsequent batteries (FIX #20). Caught ONLY by cmp-vs-solution.patch.
**Proposal:** *(1) The byte-compare of the staged tree against solution.patch is the FIRST and LAST command of every floor session — not only after reference-touching waves. (2) Lock files carry the writer PID; a lock whose PID is dead is stale and removable. (3) Assertion-text DUPLICATION across tests breaks flip-needle uniqueness — anchor every flip needle with a preceding contextual line, and audit needle counts offline before any battery run. (4) Stray test files under scratch dirs get collected by path-filtering runners — assert zero collectible test files outside the test tree before certifying.*

### B2. CERTIFICATION LOCATION + TRANSPORT
**Target:** pre_submit_gate / 04_create harness sections.
**Measured:** /tmp purges destroyed a pristine clone, an independent-implementation worktree, and 24 certification XMLs mid-project (twice). And only THROUGH-test.sh certification exercises the shipped config-loading path — the AR T6 blocker class (config load writing beside root-owned files) is structurally INVISIBLE to raw runner invocations, which is how it survived every raw-vitest certification round we ran. (The 1-in-6 middleware flake, honestly attributed, was caught by a raw 6x round — the through-test.sh rule is about fidelity to the shipped artifact, not about flake sensitivity.)
**Proposal:** *All certification artifacts live in the session scratchpad or the project tree, never /tmp. Flakiness and F2P certification execute THROUGH test.sh (the artifact that ships), comparing per-testcase outcomes across runs — never exit codes, never a raw runner invocation.*

### B3. ENV LESSONS ENCODE AS TESTABLE PROPERTIES, NOT SURFACE RECIPES
**Target:** repeat_repo template + platform_spec env section.
**Measured:** feature 1's lesson was stored as a surface recipe (".mjs, never .ts") — feature 2 obeyed it and STILL drew the same failure class via a different route (config loading writing beside root-owned files; FIX #20/AR T6). The property was "nothing may write outside tmpdirs at config-load time"; the recipe was one instance of it.
**Proposal:** *Every env lesson is stored as a PROPERTY plus a LOCAL TEST for it. For this class: run both test.sh modes against a read-only repo copy (chmod -R a-w, or as an unprivileged user) — any EACCES = the T6 class caught locally, pre-submission. Generalize: ask "what property did the platform actually enforce?" before recording any env fix.*

