# WORKFLOW CRITIQUE — why FP keeps failing, and the fixes

**Author:** the agent that just took `eclipse-langium/langium` feature #4 to approval.
**Cost of the run being critiqued:** 21 FIX waves + **5 FP panel rounds** (3 outright failures), ~67 agent runs consumed.
**Scope:** every principle below is REPO-AGNOSTIC. Nothing here depends on langium, TypeScript, or a validation feature. Where I cite langium it is as *evidence*, never as the rule.
**Status:** proposal. Nothing in `~/Desktop/olympus-workflow/` has been edited (READ-ONLY, per standing instruction).

---

## 0. THE HEADLINE

The FP documentation is **not wrong. It is unreachable at the moment it would work.**

I verified this by grep rather than memory:

```
04_create.md  (33KB, the CREATION doc)   mentions "FP_HANDBOOK"   ->  0 times
04_create.md                             mentions "atom list"     ->  0 times
/new-sub, /speed, /validate              mention "FP_HANDBOOK"    ->  0 times
FP_HANDBOOK is referenced ONLY from: FIELD_CARD, MANIFEST, pre_submit_gate, fp_kill_protocol, DOCTRINE_LEDGER
```

Every one of those referrers runs **after the artifacts already exist**. `FP_HANDBOOK.md` Part 2 Step 2 says, in the workflow's own words:

> *"Write the atom list before the prose. Enumerate every behavior you intend to gate. Then write the description FROM the atom list. Writing prose first and back-filling tests is how the promise/gate gap opens."*

My own log, line 788: *"This is langium3's stated #1 lesson and I did it in reverse: prose first, atoms extracted afterwards, gates bolted on for 8 waves."* The first reference to `FP_HANDBOOK` anywhere in my 26-wave log is at **FIX #10 — after the first FP panel had already failed.**

**So the defect is not doctrinal. It is one of SEQUENCING and ROUTING.** The workflow teaches FP as a *repair* discipline (`/fix-fp`, `fp_kill_protocol`, "run BEFORE every submit") when FP is overwhelmingly a *creation-time* property. By the time `/fix-fp` runs, the description is written, the tests are written, the reference is frozen — and the only lever still cheap (deleting a promise) now costs a full regeneration, while the expensive lever (adding a gate) is the one the panel's report literally hands you.

Everything below follows from that single misplacement.

---

## PART A — THE FP DEFECTS (priority order)

### A1. FP is filed as a REPAIR discipline; it is a CREATION discipline. `[SEVERITY: CRITICAL]`

**Evidence.** The three biggest FP artifacts are `fp_kill_protocol.md` (70KB, subtitled *"run BEFORE every submit"*), `/fix-fp` (a repair command), and `pre_submit_gate.md` (54KB, terminal). All three are downstream of creation. `04_create.md` — the doc that actually decides the FP surface, because it is where prose and tests are born — contains no FP-design step at all.

**Why this costs iterations.** FP surface is fixed at the instant you write a sentence you will not gate. Every downstream doc can only *discover* that sentence, never *prevent* it. Discovery costs a panel round; prevention costs one line of judgment.

**Concrete proof from my run.** One sentence — *"remembered ... so a later build does not repeat it"* — caused **three separate FP failures across three panels**. It was never gated by any test, because it described machinery the repo already owned rather than anything my feature promised. An atom list would have killed it before it was ever written: it had no atom, therefore no test, therefore no reason to exist.

**FIX A1 — move FP left. Add a mandatory gate to `04_create.md`, before any prose is written:**

> **CREATION GATE F — THE ATOM LIST (blocking; no description sentence may be written before this exists).**
> 1. Enumerate every behavior you intend to gate, numbered R1..Rn. Expect 30-40 for a 500-word description.
> 2. For each atom name its killing test *in the same line*. An atom with no named test is deleted, not deferred.
> 3. **Write the description FROM the numbered list.** Every sentence must cite the atom it came from.
> 4. Any sentence that cites no atom is deleted before the draft is finished.
>
> Artifact: `_artifacts/atom_list.md`. `/validate` refuses to proceed without it.

**Global, not repo-specific:** every submission on every repo has a description and a test suite. The ordering constraint between them is universal.

---

### A2. `/fix-fp` structurally BIASES toward the expensive lever `[SEVERITY: CRITICAL]`

**Evidence.** `FP_HANDBOOK.md` §1.1 states the equation and ranks the levers correctly:

```
FP SURFACE = what the PROMPT PROMISES  −  what the TESTS GATE
GATE MORE  = shrinks FP, can DESTROY solvability, high cost
PROMISE LESS = shrinks FP, costs NOTHING
```

But `/fix-fp` — the command actually invoked when a panel fails — never states this equation, never ranks the levers, and never requires you to try the free one first. It is 32 lines of ledger discipline, re-verification law, and compliance proof. Its opening instruction is to re-read experience.md; its operational core is *"gate EACH sibling"*. The word "narrow" appears once, buried inside a sentence about scope-as-armor.

**Why this costs iterations.** A panel report arrives naming a divergence. The path of least resistance — and the one `/fix-fp` describes — is to write a test for it. That closes one instance while the class stays open, and spends a solver. This is exactly the mechanism `FP_HANDBOOK` §1.1 diagnoses ("the five failed rounds only ever pulled the first lever") — but the diagnosis lives in a doc `/fix-fp` does not load.

**Measured on my run.** The two class-S narrowings cost **zero solvers** and one of them **restored 30 points of solvability** (0/13 cliff -> 30%). The gates I added cost 0-3 solvers each. Same FP outcome, opposite solvability outcome.

**FIX A2 — put a forced ordering at the TOP of `/fix-fp`, above all ledger discipline:**

> **THE LEVER ORDER (answer in writing before you touch any file).**
> `FP SURFACE = PROMISED − GATED.` For the flagged behavior, answer in this order and stop at the first YES:
> 1. **Is this behavior MY FEATURE'S promise, or machinery the repo already owns?** Repo's -> **DELETE the sentence** (free). Log as class S.
> 2. **Is the promise WIDER than the wall it protects?** -> **NARROW the quantifier** (free). Log as class N.
> 3. **Is it genuinely mine and genuinely promised?** -> only now **ADD A GATE** — and **MINE it first** (§A3).
>
> A `/fix-fp` wave that adds a gate without recording why steps 1 and 2 were both NO is **INCOMPLETE**.

**Global:** the equation is arithmetic over prose and tests. It holds for any repo.

---

### A3. The panel's REMEDY is treated as instruction, not hypothesis `[SEVERITY: HIGH]`

**Evidence.** `FP_HANDBOOK` §1.4 (LAW 5) is unambiguous: *"A panel sees one candidate; it does not see the population. Before adopting any prescribed fix, mine the last batch's patches against it. If it fails 100% of them, it is a 0-solve cliff, not a fix."* `/fix-fp` mentions mining only inside the LOCAL PRE-PLATFORM VALIDATION LAW paragraph, as a pre-submission scoreboard step — **not as a precondition on adopting the panel's proposed remedy.**

**Measured on my run.** A gate I wrote from the reference's behavior alone failed **10 of 10 real implementations** — an invented requirement, not a wall. Mining caught it; nothing else would have. My log, ledger row 60, tags this as *"the exact failure FP_HANDBOOK 1.5 exists to prevent."* I still shipped it into a wave because the command did not stop me.

**FIX A3 — one line, made blocking in `/fix-fp`:**

> **NO NEW GATE SHIPS UNMINED.** Apply the candidate gate to every implementation from the last batch before it enters a regeneration. **0 passers = cliff, do not ship** (find another close, escalate). **100% fail the new test specifically = an invented requirement**, scope it out. **0 solver cost = free closure, always ship.** Record the mined number in the ledger row; a gate with no mined number is not shipped.

---

### A4. Atom coverage is mistaken for path coverage `[SEVERITY: HIGH — this one is missing from the docs entirely]`

**Evidence.** Both `fp_kill_protocol` STEP 1 and `FP_HANDBOOK` §1.8 organise the floor around the **atomic requirement list** — mutate per atom, one mutant per atom. That is necessary and it is **not sufficient**, and no doc currently says so.

**Measured on my run (ledger row 88, my most expensive single lesson).** I proved **30/30 atoms gated** and still shipped **two FP failures**. Cause: every mutant I wrote lived in the *scheduler* — the subsystem the feature was "about" — while all three panel defects lived in the **built-ins / hooks / bookkeeping plumbing around it**. Atoms describe *what you promised*; defects live in *code the feature touches*. Those are different sets, and the second is the one the panel probes.

**FIX A4 — add a second, orthogonal quota to the executed floor (`fp_kill_protocol` §3B + `FP_HANDBOOK` §1.7):**

> **TWO QUOTAS, BOTH MANDATORY — they are orthogonal and neither implies the other.**
> - **Per ATOM:** >=1 mutant per atomic requirement (existing rule; do NOT sample — sampling lies, see A5).
> - **Per CODE PATH:** >=1 **KILLED** mutant per subsystem/file the reference diff touches, **plus every pre-existing path the feature re-routes** (hooks it now bypasses, built-ins it now reports, state it now writes).
>
> Before mutating, list the subsystems the diff touches and the paths it re-routes. **A subsystem with zero killed mutants is an ungated subsystem, regardless of atom coverage.**

The re-routing clause matters as much as the diff clause: my surviving panel dissent was a `protected` hook the new path no longer called. That hook appears in **no** diff line and maps to **no** atom.

