## PART B — NON-FP DEFECTS (same read, lower priority)

### B1. The DISCLOSURE RATCHET has no counter-force at creation `[HIGH]`

Every rule in the system adds disclosure; none removes one. The DIFFICULTY RESTORATION GATE correctly catches this **per wave** — but a project that starts over-promised is already deep in the hole before wave 1. **Fix:** run the atom list (A1) as an anti-ratchet at creation — write the *narrow* form first (`FP_HANDBOOK` LAW 2), so later waves have room to disclose without collapsing. Concretely: **budget the description to 490 words, not 500.** I sat at 499-500 for 12 waves with no headroom and had to scope out to 454 to buy room.

### B2. A wall with one observable consequence is a tripwire `[HIGH]`

The system optimises *pass rate* but never *wall shape*. My accepted batch scored 1/10 while every failing run passed 69-73 of 74 tests — and drew the only scored criticism I received: an advisory that the rate "understates broad task completion". **Fix:** at atom-list time, every intended wall must name **>=2 independent observable consequences** (different describes, different subsystems). One-test walls read as thin final-mile tripwires. Pairs with the FIELD_CARD corollary already in the docs: *if missing one requirement fails exactly one test, that test IS the wall and disclosing it ends the project.*

### B3. Verdict severity is treated as priority; it is a schedule `[HIGH — cheapest fix in this document]`

I deferred an Auto Review **MEDIUM** (*"tests do not verify the custom `thisObj` receiver is preserved"*) because it was advisory. **That exact finding came back as my final FP failure and cost a full batch.** The panel and the reviewer are looking at the same artifact through different lenses; a reviewer's low-severity note is a *preview* of a probe. **Fix, one line in `/fix-auto-review` and `/fix-fp`:**

> **An Auto Review finding at ANY severity — including MEDIUM/advisory — is a PREDICTION OF THE NEXT FP PROBE. Close it in the wave it appears. Deferring an advisory finding is how it returns priced at a full batch.**

### B4. `/fix-*` commands carry ~60% identical preamble `[MEDIUM]`

The MISSION / AUTO SELF-AUDIT / ALL-CHECKS COEXISTENCE / DILIGENCE blocks are duplicated near-verbatim across all five fix commands. The genuinely check-specific content in `/fix-fp` is roughly 6 of 32 lines. This dilutes the specific instruction and makes the docs drift apart on edit. **Fix:** extract the shared preamble to one `FIX_PREAMBLE.md`, include by reference, and let each command be *only* its distinctive method.

### B5. Reference-integrity discipline is FP-scoped but is universal `[MEDIUM]`

`cmp`-against-snapshot appears only in FP contexts. It should be a **global preflight before any regeneration of any deliverable**, on any repo. A corrupted reference is equally fatal to Quality and Agent Runs.

