## PART C — THE ONE-PAGE ANSWER TO "HOW DO WE PASS FP FIRST TIME"

Six rules. All repo-agnostic. In sequence.

1. **ATOM LIST BEFORE PROSE.** Number every behavior you will gate; name its killing test on the same line; write the description *from* the list. Delete every sentence that cites no atom. *(would have removed 3 of my 5 FP rounds)*
2. **THE OWNERSHIP QUESTION, per atom.** *"Is this MY feature's promise, or machinery the repo already owns?"* Anything the repo owns is cut before it is written. *(the single question that kills the most FP surface, at zero cost)*
3. **STATE EVERY DELIBERATE SILENCE POSITIVELY.** Not omission — an explicit sentence: *"where no declaration separates two stages, either may run first."* This is the sentence an adjudicator quotes back to overturn a dissenting judge. *(both my final overturns turned on exactly this)*
4. **TWO MUTATION QUOTAS: per atom AND per code path** — including every pre-existing path the feature re-routes. All atoms, all paths, one site at a time, no-op control survives, positive control dies. *(atom coverage alone gave me 30/30 and two FPs)*
5. **MINE EVERY GATE BEFORE SHIPPING IT.** 0 passers = cliff. 100% fail the new test = invented requirement. 0 cost = free closure. *(caught a gate that failed 10/10 real implementations)*
6. **CLOSE EVERY VERDICT FINDING AT ANY SEVERITY, IN ITS OWN WAVE.** The advisory you defer is the probe you meet next round. *(my deferred MEDIUM became my final FP failure)*

**And the honest limit, so this is not oversold:** of my 5 FP probes, **2 were free** — prose discipline (rules 1-3) would have deleted them outright. The other **3 were real defects** in the reference. Rules 4-6 do not make those disappear; they move them **off the panel and into the pre-batch floor**, where a miss costs a day instead of a batch. That is the realistic target: **FP-first-time becomes likely, not guaranteed** — and the residual failures become cheap.

