## ⛔ DEFECT A — THE DOCUMENT'S PROCEDURE CONTRADICTS ITS OWN NEWEST AND BEST LAW
### *(highest value item in this file; costs nothing but a renumber)*

**WHAT THE DOC SAYS NOW.** The executable spine runs:
`STEP 1 atoms → STEP 2 living map → STEP 3 cheat sweep → STEP 3B executed floor → STEP 4 reverse map →`
**`STEP 5 — CUT THE OVER-PROMISES`**. And the framing section near the top lists the two survival routes in this
order: *"1. **CLOSE it** … 2. **SCOPE it out**"*.

Meanwhile **LAW N1 — THE SHIELD** (*"read the description CLAUSE BY CLAUSE … If NO → **NARROW or DELETE the
clause** FIRST. Adding the test is the fallback, not the reflex"*) was appended **~100 lines BELOW the step it is
supposed to govern**, in a block after the numbered protocol ends.

**WHAT WENT WRONG (measured).** An agent executing the document top-to-bottom builds gates for **four steps**
before it is ever invited to delete a promise. I did exactly that, five rounds running. Each round I closed the
reported instance while the *class* stayed open, and the ratchet ran until one such closure measured **0 solves**.
The round that passed pulled the free lever — and the free lever is step 5 of 9 in a 70 KB document.

**This is not a doctrine gap. It is an ordering bug, and ordering is what an executing agent obeys.**

**THE FIX (exact, minimal):**
1. Renumber so the free lever is **STEP 2**: `STEP 1 ATOMS → STEP 2 THE PROMISE AUDIT (narrow/delete first) →
   STEP 3 living map over WHAT SURVIVED → STEP 4 cheat sweep → STEP 5 executed floor → …`
   The map and the floor then run over a *smaller, honest* surface, which also makes them faster.
2. Move LAW N1/N2/N3 + the class-N guardrail **into STEP 2's body**, leaving a pointer where they are now.
3. Swap the framing list to **"1. SCOPE it out (free) … 2. CLOSE it (costs solvers)"**, and add one line:
   *"Try route 1 on every finding before route 2. Route 2 is never wrong, but it is never free."*

**FIVE-BAR GATE.** Strip-the-nouns: passes (no repo noun). Fire-count **(re-verified over the full 11-debrief corpus): 7 of 11** contain an out-of-contract / prompt-silent /
scope-fence episode. On the four same-repo projects the FP-round counts are **1, 3, 5, 5** — and the **1** is the
project whose debrief says *"symmetric silence killed the only panel dissent"*, i.e. **the only one that scoped
early is the only one that passed FP on the first panel.** (I first wrote "4 of 4 … 3, 3, 5, 5"; the 1 was never
verified and is wrong. The corrected figure argues the point *better* than the wrong one did.) Existing-home: yes — this is a reorganisation of existing text, not new doctrine. Provenance:
EXECUTED-BY-ME on one project, corroborated by the corpus. Cost-of-wrong: ~zero; the worst case is that an agent
narrows a clause it should have gated, which the existing class-N guardrail (reverse-direction audit) already
catches. **VERDICT: ADOPT — structural, no new doctrine.**

