## ⛔ DEFECT C — SILENCE IS DOCTRINE'D AS OMISSION AND SYMMETRY, NEVER AS **POSITIVE PROSE**
### *(this is the one I would fix second, and it is a genuine CORRECTION to an existing law)*

**WHAT THE DOC SAYS NOW.** §3 SYMMETRIC SILENCE: *"UNSPECIFIED IN PROMPT ⇔ UNIMPLEMENTED IN REFERENCE … for every
behavior you scope OUT of the prompt, scope it out of the REFERENCE too … **asymmetric silence invites the probe;
symmetric silence kills it**."* The strings **"positively" and "state the silence" appear zero times.**

**WHERE THAT IS WRONG (measured counterexample).** On the accepted project, the disputed behaviour was
**implemented in the reference** and therefore *asymmetric* by the doc's definition — the condition under which
the doc predicts you get probed and lose. A judge did build a probe, and it **genuinely discriminated**
(candidate 1 space, reference 5). **The dissent was overturned anyway**, because the description carried a
*positively stated* scope clause the adjudicator could quote verbatim:

> *"the prompt scopes priority arbitration to align-vs-ordinary (**'when an ordinary one on that same side wants
> it too'**) … and says nothing about two same-side appended actions converging on one target."*

The sibling langium project won **both** of its final probes the same way, on two positively stated silences:
*"either may run first"* and *"left as it is today."* Its own debrief calls this *"the single highest-leverage
sentence-level technique in the whole project."*

**THE PRINCIPLE, repo-agnostically.** An adjudicator adjudicates on **quotable text**. They can quote a sentence;
**they cannot quote an omission.** Symmetric silence works by making the probe *undiscriminating* — it needs the
reference to share the gap. Positive scoping works by making the probe *out-of-contract* — it holds **even when
the reference implements the behaviour**, which is the strictly larger and more useful case.

**THE FIX.**
1. Add **LAW N4 — STATE THE SILENCE POSITIVELY.** *"Do not merely omit a behaviour you do not intend to gate;
   write one sentence that positively bounds the contract ('either order is allowed', 'left as it is today',
   'for direct children only'). An adjudicator can quote a sentence at a dissenting judge; they cannot quote an
   omission."*
2. **Correct §3**: demote symmetric silence to the *fallback* used when the behaviour cannot be positively bounded
   in prose, and delete the implication that asymmetric silence necessarily loses. Replace with: *"asymmetric
   silence loses only when it is also UNSTATED. Asymmetric + positively scoped survived a genuinely
   discriminating probe (measured, 2026-08-10)."*

**FIVE-BAR GATE.** Strip-the-nouns: passes. Fire-count **(re-verified): 2 of 11**, both langium → **n=2 same-repo → CANDIDATE, not GLOBAL.** Count unchanged
from my original claim; only the denominator was wrong. Existing-home: §3 + the class-N block. Provenance: one
EXECUTED-BY-ME, one READ-SECOND-HAND. Cost-of-wrong: **the real risk** — an agent could positively state a scope
it *does* gate, creating prose/test disagreement. Mitigated by the existing class-N reverse-direction audit, which
must be cited inside LAW N4.
**VERDICT: ADOPT-AS-EXTENSION with honest scope ("measured on two projects, same repo — watch for a third").**
I flag deliberately: this is the item I am most confident about *practically* and least able to prove *globally*.

