## ⛔ DEFECT E — "THE REFERENCE MUST BE GOLD" IS INTROSPECTION, NOT A METHOD

**WHAT THE DOC SAYS NOW.** *"Before submit, per requirement ask: 'does my reference do this CORRECTLY, or did I
take a shortcut the panel will find?'"* — and, correctly, *"no scope sentence saves a genuine reference bug."*

**WHAT WENT WRONG (measured).** My project shipped **three genuine reference bugs** into panel rounds:
edit-ordering, zero-width-edit stacking, and order-based claim resolution. **Zero of the three were found by
asking myself that question.** All three were found by **constructing a cross-product cell and executing it
against the reference**. Asking a builder whether their own reference is correct returns "yes" — that is what
building it meant.

The doc has the CLAUSE CROSS-PRODUCT LAW, but it frames the sweep as a **test-coverage** activity: *"record each
intersection as covered / scoped-out / NEW GAP."* All three of those outcomes are about *tests*. There is no
outcome for *"I ran this cell and the reference did something I did not expect."*

**THE FIX.** Make the cross-product sweep **execute against the reference before any test is authored**, and add a
fourth outcome to the intersection log:

```
covered  |  scoped-out  |  NEW GAP  |  REFERENCE SURPRISED ME  <- new
```
A `REFERENCE SURPRISED ME` cell is **either a reference bug or an unstated requirement** and must be resolved as
one or the other *before* a test is written for it. Writing the test first freezes the bug into the contract.

**FIVE-BAR GATE.** Strip-the-nouns: passes. Fire-count **(re-verified by READING every hit, not just counting them): 7 of 11 fire, and 1 is an explicit
