## ⛔ DEFECT G — `/fix-fp` HAS NO ENTRY TRIAGE: EVERY FINDING GETS THE FULL 70 KB

**WHAT THE COMMAND SAYS NOW.** *"READ NOW: fp_kill_protocol.md IN FULL … Iterate the fix 3× through the whole
protocol before resubmitting."*

**WHY THAT IS WRONG.** Findings come in three kinds and **the correct first move is different for each**:

| finding class | correct first move | cost | how you know |
|---|---|---|---|
| **(a) out-of-contract** — the probe demands more than you promised | **NARROW the clause** | ~10 min, 0 solvers | no sentence in the prompt forces the behaviour |
| **(b) real gap** in a behaviour you genuinely promised | **GATE it — but mine the gate first** | 0–3 solvers | a prompt sentence demands it |
| **(c) reference bug** | **FIX THE REFERENCE** | varies | the reference itself gets the cell wrong |

Routing (a) into the full close-the-gap protocol is how five rounds became five rounds: the protocol is *built*
to help you add a gate, so given a finding, it adds one. The doc **already has this taxonomy** — but only inside
the SAME-CHECK-FAILING-TWICE law, i.e. as something you reach for on the **second** failure of the same check.

**THE FIX.** Make it **STEP 1 of `/fix-fp`, on the first failure**: *reproduce the probe by execution → classify
a/b/c → route.* Only classes (b) and (c) open the full protocol. And keep the doc's own excellent warning attached
to (a): an overturned probe **must not** be given a test — that is the inverse reject.

**FIVE-BAR GATE.** Strip-the-nouns: passes. Fire-count **(THIRD pass — my "9 of 11" was INVALID).** That grep matched *"a probe was OVERTURNED"*, which is
evidence that **scoping worked**, not evidence that **gating was wrongly attempted first**. Those are different
propositions and I counted one as the other — the identical loose-term error I flag in Defect I, committed twice
