## PART 1 — WHAT SURVIVES REVALIDATION

### ⛔ S-1. **ASSERTION BLINDNESS — the decorative-flip rule exists, I ran it, and it did not catch this.**
*(Strongest surviving item. Better founded now than in v1.)*

**What exists.** Line 353, THE DECORATIVE-ASSERTION TEST: *"flip the expected value to something wrong and
re-run — suite still green = the assertion asserts nothing (is_ok, len>0, contains-without-exact-value are the
usual suspects)."*

**I ran it. 15 of 15 assertions were load-bearing.** The blindness survived anyway, and a human reviewer found
a real requirement violation my suite was structurally incapable of detecting.

**Why the existing rule cannot catch this class.** Flipping an expected *value* catches an assertion that
asserts *nothing*. My assertions asserted plenty — they were blind to a **different key set**. The framework's
default equality treats *key present with an undefined value* as *identical to key absent*, so `{a:1}` compared
EQUAL to `{a:1, phantom:undefined}`. Deletions in my feature were encoded exactly that way. Flip any value in
that object and the test dies correctly; add a phantom key and it stays green. **Value-sensitivity and
shape-sensitivity are different properties, and only the first is currently tested.**

Same root cause hit three tools in one project: the assertion library ignored the distinction, the serialiser
dropped it, and the repo's own map helper deleted on it.

**Nearest existing rule (v3 check, cited for honesty):** `pre_submit_gate.md:165` — *"Sentinel value (undefined
vs null) not stated -> unfair."* That governs the PROMPT side (state your sentinel or the test is unfair). It
does not govern the ASSERTION side (can your comparison SEE the sentinel). Adjacent, not overlapping.

**PROPOSED — one clause appended to the DECORATIVE-ASSERTION TEST:**
> Flipping the VALUE proves the assertion is not vacuous. It does NOT prove the comparison is SHAPE-sensitive.
> Additionally feed your comparison the pair it must distinguish — present-but-empty vs absent, zero vs unset,
> empty collection vs null — and assert it reports them DIFFERENT. A comparison that cannot separate a pair your
> contract separates is BLIND, and every row it backs is unproven regardless of its RED-PROOF.

**Bar 1 strip-the-nouns:** passes. **Bar 2 fire-count:** 0/9 pass 1, **1/9** pass 2 (dolt). **Bar 3:** existing
home = the decorative-assertion test → **ADOPT-AS-EXTENSION, one clause.** **Bar 4:** EXECUTED-BY-ME, 3
instances, 1 project. **Bar 5:** two extra lines per audit; no disclosure, no Restoration-Gate debt.
**My honest read:** the fire-count says project-local; I think it is under-counted because a blind comparison is
invisible by construction and therefore under-reported in debriefs. **The bar should win unless your terms fire
higher.** Suggested terms: `deep.?equal|equality|sentinel|empty vs|null vs|undefined|absent`.

