# THE FP HANDBOOK

**How to make the False-Positive panel pass on the first try, without killing solvability.**

Derived from a project that failed FP **five consecutive times** and then passed **3/3 genuine** — every
claim below is backed by a measurement from those runs, not by reasoning. Written 2026-08-10 off
`eclipse-langium/langium` batch 28 (10 runs, 80 artifacts, all read), plus the four preceding FP panels.

Three sections:
1. **UNIVERSAL** — applies to every repo, every feature, every submission.
2. **THE DESIGN METHOD** — how to build a feature that is FP-proof *before* the first test is written.
3. **REPO APPENDIX: langium** — reusable facts for other submissions on this repo.

---

# PART 1 — UNIVERSAL

## 1.1 The one equation that explains every FP failure

```
        FP SURFACE  =  what the PROMPT PROMISES  −  what the TESTS GATE
```

An FP verdict needs a probe that is **fair AND prompt-grounded**. A judge who finds a divergence must
still show the prompt *demanded* that behavior. So the surface has **two** edges, and you can shrink it
from either side:

| lever | effect on FP | effect on solvability | cost |
|---|---|---|---|
| **GATE MORE** — add a killing test | shrinks | **can destroy it** (every added gate is another wall) | high, sometimes fatal |
| **PROMISE LESS** — narrow the prose | shrinks | **none** | free |

**The five failed rounds only ever pulled the first lever.** Each FP report was answered with "add a test
for the reported corner", which (a) closed one instance while the *class* stayed open, and (b) ratcheted
difficulty until a fix measured **0 solves**. The round that passed pulled **both** levers in one
regeneration.

### The measurement that proves the second lever wins

Batch 28 had three passing runs. **Two of them diverged from the reference and were still ruled genuine —
purely because of prompt narrowness:**

- **Run 9** (panel "Nova·2") dedups converging same-side appends by *source order* instead of priority. A
  judge built a discriminating probe, ran it, and the candidate genuinely failed while the reference
  passed. **Adjudicator: genuine pass.** Reason, quoted: *"the prompt scopes priority arbitration to
  align-vs-ordinary ('when an ordinary one on that same side wants it too') ... and says nothing about two
  same-side appended align actions from different source nodes converging on one target."*
- **Run 5/10** (panel "Nova·1") diverges on fractional `spaces` and `tabSize: 0`. **Adjudicator: genuine
  pass** — *"neither of which the prompt constrains ... polish, not correctness gaps."*

The words **"on that same side"** were added the wave before as a scope-narrowing edit. That five-word
clause is what converted a would-be FP into a genuine pass. **Silence is a shield. Every sentence you
write is a liability you must gate.**

> **LAW 1 — THE SHIELD.** Before shipping, read the prompt sentence by sentence and ask of each clause:
> *do I have a killing test for exactly this?* If no — **delete or narrow the clause**. Do not add the
> test reflexively; adding is the expensive lever, narrowing is the free one.

> **LAW 2 — NEVER PROMISE A SUPERSET.** "priority settles which action claims a gap" promises priority
> arbitration *everywhere*. "priority settles it when an ordinary one **on that same side** wants it too"
> promises it in one place. Same difficulty, a fraction of the surface. Write the narrow form first.

## 1.2 Where FP holes actually live: the cross-product

All four FP findings on this project had the identical shape, and it was never the shape being reported:

| round | the rule I tested | the axis I did not cross it with |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | widest gap wins | widest-gap member ≠ widest-content member |
| 2 | prepend enrols region nodes | a node whose *token type is named* like a comment |
| 3 | append skips comments | the comment **begins its own line** |
| 4 | claim ownership / priority | a **line-changing** ordinary action; **unequal** priorities |
| 5 | blank line divides a group | the blank line holds **only whitespace** |

**Every single one lived at the cross-product of two rules, not inside one rule.** Testing each rule once
is what a careful person does, and it is exactly what leaves the surface open.

> **LAW 3 — TEST THE MATRIX, NOT THE LIST.** Enumerate your rules as axes and probe the *cells*. For each
> pair of rules that can touch the same object, ask: can both apply at once? What are the sub-cases (from
> the front / from behind, higher / lower / equal priority, empty / non-empty, same / different group)?
> Probe every cell against the reference **before** writing a single test. Cells where the reference
> surprises you are either a reference bug or an unstated requirement — both are FP holes.

This project found **three real reference bugs** this way (edit ordering, zero-width edit stacking,
order-based claim resolution). None were found by reading the code; all three by constructing the cell.

## 1.3 The highest-yield wall class: ORDER OF RESOLUTION

This is the most valuable single finding in the handbook, because the same wall separated solvers from
failers **and** was FP-proof at the same time.

The feature had two operations: **pick the winning claim** and **aggregate the widest request**. Both
orders compile, both pass almost every test:

```
resolve-then-aggregate   (correct)   one claim per member  ->  max across MEMBERS
aggregate-then-resolve   (wrong)     max across CLAIMS     ->  then pick a winner
```

Measured across the 10 real implementations:

| | per-member claim aggregation | result |
|---|---|---|
| runs 2, 3, 4, 6, 7, 8 | `member.spaces = Math.max(member.spaces, spaces)` (or `.reduce(max)`, or a plural `requests[]`) | **all failed** |
| runs 5, 9, 10 | no per-member merge — one claim per member, max only across the segment | **all solved** |

Nothing else separated them. Architecture was identical (all 10 independently found the two-phase
preview design). LOC did not correlate (solvers 707 / 491 / 579; failers up to 641). **Effort did not
correlate**: solver median completion 52.8k vs failer 48.0k tokens — a 10% gap, i.e. noise.

Why this class is ideal:

- **It is a genuine invariant**, not a constant to look up — so it is not "unfair".
- **The wrong order is a superset behavior**: identical output on every input except one constructed
  cell. Local tests an agent writes for itself will never catch it.
- **It is one sentence to state**, so it is fair and it is gateable in both directions.
- **It cannot be reached by guessing**; it has to be reasoned about.

> **LAW 4 — HUNT FOR ORDER-OF-RESOLUTION INVARIANTS.** In any feature with two composable operations
> (resolve/aggregate, dedup/sort, filter/clamp, validate/normalize, expand/merge), one order is correct
> and the other is silently wrong. That is your major wall and your FP gate at the same time. Ask early:
> *"which two operations here commute in the common case but not in a constructible one?"*

## 1.4 Never take a panel's remedy on trust

The FP panel on round 4 diagnosed the defect precisely **and prescribed the wrong fix**:

> *"The fix is to pick the spaces from the priority-winning claim (ties -> later action), mirroring the
> collector's own existingPriority<=priority rule."*

I implemented exactly that. It built clean and kept the whole suite green. Then I mined all 10 real
implementations against it: **no implementation passed both directions, and the two that passed the new
gates each failed an older one — the priority doctrine scored ZERO solves.** The same matrix showed the
survivors were *front-ownership* implementations, which is what the prompt already said and what the
untouched reference already did. I reverted and shipped front-ownership instead.

> **LAW 5 — DIAGNOSIS IS EVIDENCE, REMEDY IS A HYPOTHESIS.** A panel sees one candidate; it does not see
> the population. Before adopting any prescribed fix, mine the last batch's patches against it. **If it
> fails 100% of them, it is a 0-solve cliff, not a fix.**

## 1.5 Mine every new gate before shipping it

The single cheapest insurance in this whole system. You already have N real implementations from the
previous batch. Applying a candidate gate to all of them costs one script and answers the only question
that matters: *does at least one real implementation still pass everything?*

```
for each run: clean tree -> apply agent's solution-patch -> apply YOUR test.patch -> run the suite
```

Outcomes and what they mean:

| mining result | meaning | action |
|---|---|---|
| **0 runs pass everything** | cliff | do not ship; find a different close (escalate) |
| **1+ runs pass everything** | calibrated wall | ship |
| **100% fail the NEW test specifically** | the gate demands something no architecture produces | scope it out (class S) or it is a reference bug |
| **the new test costs zero solves** | free FP closure | always ship |

The whitespace-only-line gate added in the final audit was measured at **zero cost** — no implementation
failed it, so closing that hole was free. The front-claim gate cost one run that was itself carrying the
flagged defect. That is the difference between a gate that removes a false positive and a gate that
removes a solver, and **you cannot tell them apart without mining.**

## 1.6 Reachability is CONSTRUCTED, never assumed

Two suspected holes, same audit, opposite verdicts — settled only by trying to build them:

- **Whitespace-only line counts as blank** (real hole). A whitespace-only line normally cannot survive:
  the formatter rewrites inter-token whitespace, so the obvious input produces a truly empty line. It
  survives **inside a comment**, whose interior is never rewritten. Constructed, divergence confirmed,
  test added.
- **CRLF counted as two line breaks** (not a hole). Three constructions attempted — plain CRLF endings,
  CRLF inside a comment, CRLF inside the one gap alignment leaves alone — and **no produced document
  retained a `\r` pair**. The mutated path cannot be reached, so it cannot diverge.

> **LAW 6.** "This looks like a hole" and "this is a hole" are separated by an executed construction. Try
> three ways to reach it. If all three fail, it is unobservable and you write down the three attempts.

## 1.7 The executed floor, and how to read survivors

Maintain a **mutation battery**: N single-edit mutants of your reference, each violating one behavior,
each run against the full suite. A mutant that survives is a behavior your tests do not enforce.

Non-negotiable hygiene, all learned the hard way:

- **Rebuild inside `finally`.** A battery that restores sources but not the build grades the *next*
  mutants against the *last* mutant's compiled output. This produced four phantom "defects".
- **Restore-verify byte-identical** at the end, and assert it.
- **Include a no-op control mutant that MUST survive** — it proves the battery is not trivially green.
- **Assert your mutation actually applied**: `assert src.count(needle) == 1` and `assert after != before`.
  A silent no-op once reported "43/43 PASS" for a cheat probe that never modified the file.
- Build failure / no junit / zero tests collected = **UNRESOLVED**, never "killed".

**Reading survivors — the rule that keeps this from becoming infinite work:**

> **LAW 7 — A SURVIVING MUTANT IS AN FP HOLE ONLY IF A *DESCRIBED* REQUIREMENT STOPS BEING ENFORCED.**

Classify every survivor into exactly one of: **control** (must survive) / **unobservable** (three failed
constructions) / **inert** (the change cannot alter output — e.g. enrolling an element that a later rule
discards anyway) / **undescribed** (defensive branch: zero-size inputs, inverted ranges, redundant sorts —
no prompt sentence maps to it) / **registered silence** (deliberately scoped out, written down). Anything
that does not fit one of those five is a hole.

## 1.8 The audit protocol (run this before every batch)

1. **ATOMS.** Split the prompt into atomic requirements — one per assertable behavior. Expect 30–40 for a
   500-word description. For each, name the killing test. **A missing name is a hole.**
2. **REVERSE DIRECTION.** Sweep every test for assertions the prompt does not state. Those are *unfair
   tests*, the other failure mode, and reviewers score them at band 1.
3. **EXECUTED FLOOR.** Run the battery. Triage survivors by Law 7.
4. **MATRIX.** For each pair of rules that can touch one object, probe the cells against the reference.
5. **CHEAT HUNT.** Try to pass the suite without implementing the feature (stub the API, hardcode the
   fixtures, no-op the entry point). Anything green is a hole.
6. **MINE.** Apply the final test.patch to every implementation from the last batch. Confirm ≥1 passes.
7. **SOLVABILITY.** State the measured number, not a projection.

Finish with a verdict: **FP-CLEAN (evidence per item)** or **the exact holes + the fix for each**. "Probably
fine" is not a verdict.

## 1.9 What "genuine solve" actually means — and how to make it likely

The judge's bar, from the batch-28 evals, is:

```
baseline_passed = true
new_tests_passed = true
no test manipulation
the implementation GENERALIZES (not fixture-shaped)
```

All ten runs were rated `difficulty: challenging`, all seven failures were
`was_mentioned_in_description = true` and `was_inferable_from_new_tests = false`. **Zero unfair verdicts.**
That is the target state, and it comes from three properties:

1. **Every gated behavior is stated** — so a failure is a missed requirement, never an ambush.
2. **Nothing is stated that is not gated** — so an out-of-contract divergence cannot be held against a
   passer (Law 1).
3. **At least one real implementation passes everything** — proven by mining, not hoped for.

You do **not** prevent agents from having out-of-contract divergences. Run 9 shipped with one and passed.
You make divergences **irrelevant** by not promising the behavior they diverge on.

## 1.10 Calibration signals that are real, and ones that are not

From batch 28 (10 runs, all artifacts parsed):

| signal | value | reading |
|---|---|---|
| genuine pass rate | **3/10 = 30%** | the sweet spot; band is 30–40%, hard cap 40% |
| batch median completion tokens | **50.4k** | healthy (≥~120k median = too hard) |
| solver vs failer effort | 52.8k vs 48.0k | **effort does not distinguish** — do not diagnose from it |
| `trajectory.total_steps` | `None` / constant | **useless**, a platform-preview artifact |
| UI msgs median | 74 | the msg targets are folklore; ignore against a 30% all-genuine batch |
| top failure cluster | **86% of failing runs** | see below |

**The FDI nuance, corrected here.** The rule "top cluster ≥70% of failing runs = CLIFF" fired at 86% — yet
the batch scored 30% with three genuine passes. The rule is incomplete on its own:

> **LAW 8 — FDI NEEDS THE SECOND QUESTION.** A dominant cluster is a **cliff** only if *no* run passes
> that test. If some runs pass it and others fail it, a high share means you have found the **one decisive
> major wall** — which is exactly what a well-calibrated task looks like. Always report
> `top-cluster share` **together with** `how many runs passed that same test`.

## 1.11 The disclosure ratchet, and the only safe levers

Every rule in this system adds disclosure; none removes it. A fully compliant project drifts *easier*
every wave, and the drift looks like diligence. Classify every solvability lever before pulling it:

| class | lever | FP-safe? |
|---|---|---|
| **D** | disclose the behavior; killing test unchanged | **yes**, by construction |
| **R** | make the seam discoverable in the repo | yes |
| **S** | symmetric scope-out — drop from prose **and** tests together | yes |
| **W** | delete/loosen a test, widen an assertion | **BANNED** — that IS the false positive |
| **X** | weaken the reference | banned |

A disclosure whose killing test is intact is **not** an FP hole. FP means *"passes without doing the
task"*, never *"the task was explained"*. Keep a **solvability ledger** — {what was disclosed, which
requirement, which test still gates it} — and never close an FP finding by deleting a ledgered lever.

And pair every disclosure with a wall in the same regeneration, or prove by census that a major still
stands. Otherwise the project ratchets toward trivial.

