# PART 2 — THE DESIGN METHOD (do this before writing any test)

**Step 1 — Pick a feature with a resolution ORDER, not just an output.** Look for two operations that
compose. That is your major wall (Law 4).

**Step 2 — Write the atom list before the prose.** Enumerate every behavior you intend to gate. Then write
the description *from the atom list*. Every sentence traces to an atom; every atom gets a test. Writing
prose first and back-filling tests is how the promise/gate gap opens.

**Step 3 — Build the rule matrix and probe every cell against your reference.** Do this *before* the
suite exists. Cells where the reference surprises you: fix the reference or delete the promise.

**Step 4 — For every cell you will NOT gate, remove its promise from the prose.** This is the shield. Be
ruthless; you are deleting future FP findings for free.

**Step 5 — Gate each remaining cell in BOTH directions.** One-directional gates are passed by
implementations that always pick one side.

**Step 6 — Build the battery from the atom list**, one mutant per atom, plus a control.

**Step 7 — Mine the gate set against real implementations.** Ship only with ≥1 full passer.

**Step 8 — Record deliberate silences.** Anything you chose not to gate goes in a written registry so a
future wave does not "helpfully" gate it and cliff the batch.

