-- RANDMLY FOUND THIS ON THE DEV CHAT (DISCORD CHAT OF OLYMPUS LOOK INTO IT TO MIGHT BE HELPFUL):

One of the biggest things you need to focus on is the False Positive (FP) check. Your goal is to make sure there are no gaps in your test suite that would allow a buggy or partially implemented solution to pass. The best way to avoid this is by writing comprehensive tests that cover all the important behaviors and edge cases.

A good place to start is the Test Fairness quality check. Besides checking whether the task is fair, it also provides 💡 suggestions for improving your tests. Go through these suggestions carefully and address as many as possible. They are not always correct, so if you believe a suggestion is a false positive, it's completely fine to skip it. Ideally, you should continue until there are either no suggestions left or the remaining ones are genuinely incorrect.

Another useful check is the Verifier Completeness Audit (VCA). During your first few iterations, this is a great way to identify major gaps in your test suite before spending a lot of tokens refining smaller details. Since starters have limited token budgets, finding and fixing the largest issues early is much more efficient.

When you move to the quality checks, avoid running every check at once. Your initial goal should be to pass the prechecks and then focus primarily on Test Fairness. Use VCA during the early iterations, which will naturally require running the verifier solution a few times. You should also run the Solution Quality check a few times until your reference solution consistently receives a 3/3 in both categories. Once you're confident the solution is complete and high quality, you can spend most of your remaining iterations focusing on Test Fairness.

To reduce the number of iterations needed, make sure your problem description, tests, and reference solution are all aligned before you begin refining the task. Remove unnecessary information, avoid requirement leaks, and ensure everything describes the same observable behavior.

A technique that helps significantly is to break your reference solution down into states. Think of each state as a milestone where a specific goal has been achieved. Once you've identified these states, list all the functionality required within each one, including helper functions, edge-case handling, validation logic, and other behaviors. Then verify that your test suite covers every one of them. This makes it much harder for an incorrect implementation to slip through your tests while still passing.

After you've covered each individual state, step back and look at the task as a whole. Consider how the different pieces interact and whether there are any additional scenarios that only appear when multiple components work together. If you discover one, add a test for it.

Whenever possible, write tests that verify observable behavior rather than private implementation details. This keeps the task fair while still ensuring the required functionality is implemented correctly.

Finally, if the Solution Quality check points out an issue in your reference solution, don't just fix the solution itself. Ask whether that bug could also appear in a participant's implementation. If the answer is yes, consider adding a test for it so the same mistake can't slip through in future submissions.